Ladybird’s Unicode VM searches skip surrogate interiors, even when the
pattern can match empty input. LibJS’s general lastIndex path also maps
an interior position to the pair’s start, without retrying on failure.

V8 and SpiderMonkey advance Unicode searches by whole code points in
RegExpMacroAssembler::UnanchoredAdvance(), which matches Ladybird’s
search behavior. V8’s Compiler::Assemble() also normalizes the starting
position before matching, without an unadjusted-position fallback.

SpiderMonkey’s Compiler::OptionallyStepBackToLeadSurrogate() retains a
second alternative at the unadjusted index. WebKit’s InputStream::next()
advances its interpreter’s search by one code unit. Ladybird differs
from those paths to follow RegExpBuiltinExec: Unicode matching operates
on code points, so a surrogate interior is never an input character.
